[SPEAKER_00]: Generally, one's assumptions about what's... about speech.

365
00:21:57,965 --> 00:22:11,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Speech is generally assumed to involve, kind of, me breaking out, breaking forth, pouring out.

366
00:22:11,716 --> 00:22:11,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

367
00:22:11,956 --> 00:22:13,697
[SPEAKER_00]: And me always being mental, basically.

368
00:22:13,837 --> 00:22:14,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, the mind.

369
00:22:14,538 --> 00:22:14,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

370
00:22:14,718 --> 00:22:16,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have three ways of engaging.

371
00:22:17,760 --> 00:22:19,661
[SPEAKER_01]: By body, by speech, and by mind.

372
00:22:20,807 --> 00:22:22,928
[SPEAKER_01]: My body needs to be restrained beforehand.

373
00:22:23,469 --> 00:22:25,230
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind is too subtle to see directly.

374
00:22:26,531 --> 00:22:29,933
[SPEAKER_01]: But the middle bit, the converging point, is where the speech is.

375
00:22:30,573 --> 00:22:33,575
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically where the assumption takes... where the perversion takes place.

376
00:22:33,595 --> 00:22:34,896
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I call it a converging point.

377
00:22:35,576 --> 00:22:39,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's... See, the speech in itself is physical.

378
00:22:40,883 --> 00:22:42,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Sounds uttered by your mouth.

379
00:22:42,544 --> 00:22:43,105
[SPEAKER_01]: It's bodily.

380
00:22:43,485 --> 00:22:45,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it is bodily.

381
00:22:45,707 --> 00:22:46,448
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very bodily.

382
00:22:46,488 --> 00:22:47,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's also...

383
00:22:47,269 --> 00:22:47,989
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a domain.

384
00:22:48,770 --> 00:22:49,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

385
00:22:49,270 --> 00:22:49,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

386
00:22:49,631 --> 00:22:51,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a hybrid domain, so to speak.

387
00:22:51,292 --> 00:22:52,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

388
00:22:52,333 --> 00:22:54,115
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's the first one to cease, by the way.

389
00:22:54,876 --> 00:22:58,379
[SPEAKER_01]: The body doesn't cease up until like 4th jhāna.

390
00:22:59,340 --> 00:23:01,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, that's a digression.

391
00:23:01,342 --> 00:23:04,845
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, it's another type of engagement.

392
00:23:06,529 --> 00:23:15,640
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you engage through your physical actions when you assume the ownership of it, when you're projected towards pleasure from the senses, you assume pleasure in these objects.

393
00:23:15,660 --> 00:23:16,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I act, I get them.

394
00:23:17,002 --> 00:23:18,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already a perversion.

395
00:23:18,564 --> 00:23:22,308
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're basically moving out towards the pleasure out there with your body.

396
00:23:22,508 --> 00:23:22,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

397
00:23:23,189 --> 00:23:23,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

398
00:23:23,890 --> 00:23:26,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you assume that to come first.

399
00:23:26,432 --> 00:23:27,793
[SPEAKER_00]: You think the pleasure is over there.

400
00:23:28,033 --> 00:23:28,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

401
00:23:28,313 --> 00:23:30,215
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have to engage bodily with it.

402
00:23:30,315 --> 00:23:33,678
[SPEAKER_01]: And body engaging with it is you accessing that pleasure.

403
00:23:33,698 --> 00:23:34,219
[SPEAKER_01]: You're getting it.

404
00:23:34,259 --> 00:23:34,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

405
00:23:34,479 --> 00:23:35,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

406
00:23:35,099 --> 00:23:36,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

407
00:23:36,861 --> 00:23:37,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with the speech.

408
00:23:38,062 --> 00:23:43,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Just now, it's not so much into the senses, but it's more between the body and the point of view.

409
00:23:44,407 --> 00:23:45,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Between the mind and the body.

410
00:23:45,768 --> 00:23:47,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Between the verbal thinking and pondering.

411
00:23:50,428 --> 00:24:09,576
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you think, like everybody, when you speak, the whole notion behind, or even before you speak, the notion behind is, I have thoughts, I have sentences, I have words in my mind, I have the whole voice, and then that kind of flows into the speech out there, and it's the same thing.

412
00:24:11,177 --> 00:24:11,977
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's impossible.

413
00:24:12,397 --> 00:24:16,679
[SPEAKER_01]: How can a mental thought flow into the material body?

414
00:24:17,936 --> 00:24:18,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Two separate domains.

415
00:24:18,877 --> 00:24:23,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Two separate domains that are simultaneously present and never occur without each other.

416
00:24:23,901 --> 00:24:25,722
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the root of the confusion.

417
00:24:26,122 --> 00:24:28,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing the nature of that particular saṅkhara.

418
00:24:28,704 --> 00:24:28,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

419
00:24:29,325 --> 00:24:32,147
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the definition of ignorance.

420
00:24:32,307 --> 00:24:34,189
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why one breaks into speech.

421
00:24:35,189 --> 00:24:40,273
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even when you speak, your thoughts can never become the bodily sounds.

422
00:24:40,834 --> 00:24:41,014
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

423
00:24:42,047 --> 00:24:44,648
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're always simultaneous with it.

424
00:24:44,708 --> 00:24:47,928
[SPEAKER_01]: There are no bodily sounds without thoughts being there present.

425
00:24:47,988 --> 00:24:54,130
[SPEAKER_00]: But, sort of phenomenologically, when you're speaking, that speech sort of takes the place of your thought.

426
00:24:54,170 --> 00:24:54,810
[SPEAKER_00]: That does...

427
00:24:55,130 --> 00:24:56,610
[SPEAKER_01]: I see that's the assumption of Bhutuja.

428
00:24:56,910 --> 00:24:57,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, okay.

429
00:24:57,730 --> 00:24:58,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Who hasn't developed jhānas.

430
00:24:59,271 --> 00:25:07,293
[SPEAKER_01]: You think that your speech is taking... How can... How can a physical utterance of these sounds, these vibrations, take place of your thought?

431
00:25:07,373 --> 00:25:07,613
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

432
00:25:08,193 --> 00:25:08,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

433
00:25:10,299 --> 00:25:19,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Not having fully discerned that, to that extent, you still own and possess, through perversion, the domain of the body, which is the domain of the senses.

434
00:25:20,342 --> 00:25:32,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully discern the separateness of it, inherently, impossible to join together, you will then experience the relief of the pressure of the senses that were exhibited on the body.

435
00:25:33,245 --> 00:25:34,325
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is the first jhāna.

436
00:25:34,625 --> 00:25:36,846
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the speech will cease in the first jhāna.

437
00:25:38,649 --> 00:25:39,909
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to cultivate that.

438
00:25:40,289 --> 00:25:44,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensory strength needs to be done first, and maintained throughout, of course.

439
00:25:44,791 --> 00:25:50,292
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, practice anchoring of your peripheral awareness, as we spoke before, as in recollecting of your mindfulness.

440
00:25:50,693 --> 00:25:55,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Recollecting the fact that you're not speaking while you're thinking verbally.

441
00:25:55,134 --> 00:25:58,315
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're cultivating, basically,

442
00:25:59,355 --> 00:26:06,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Discernment of the two simultaneous domains, that they're inseparable from each other, but you're not confounding them anymore, because you...

443
00:26:06,558 --> 00:26:06,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Speech and thought.

444
00:26:07,279 --> 00:26:07,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

445
00:26:07,879 --> 00:26:09,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Speech and the thought.

446
00:26:10,160 --> 00:26:14,502
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they're like vāci-sankhāra, the determination of your speech, because it's inseparable from it.

447
00:26:17,903 --> 00:26:18,643
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's it's that.

448
00:26:18,804 --> 00:26:20,845
[SPEAKER_00]: It's it's not discerning...

449
00:26:20,885 --> 00:26:26,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, just to Vīṭaka-vichāra, thinking and pondering is the determination for speech.

450
00:26:26,407 --> 00:26:30,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking and pondering is anything... You can't have speech without thinking and pondering.

451
00:26:30,490 --> 00:26:31,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

452
00:26:31,130 --> 00:26:31,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

453
00:26:31,630 --> 00:26:32,411
[SPEAKER_01]: They're in separate forms.

454
00:26:32,471 --> 00:26:38,814
[SPEAKER_01]: But, see, in that practice of anchoring your mindfulness in the absence of speech, the mouth that's factually

455
00:26:39,757 --> 00:26:41,739
[SPEAKER_01]: quiet, not producing sound.

456
00:26:42,280 --> 00:26:45,884
[SPEAKER_01]: You can then understand the extent of thinking and pondering.

457
00:26:46,145 --> 00:26:46,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

458
00:26:46,465 --> 00:26:47,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Without confusing it for speech.

459
00:26:48,247 --> 00:26:50,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, the the... That's why silence

460
00:26:51,947 --> 00:27:01,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Physical solitude and not careless chit-chat and so on is absolutely necessary and the Buddha could have not been, like, cleared and have a more hard-line stance on it than he did.

461
00:27:01,754 --> 00:27:01,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

462
00:27:02,454 --> 00:27:10,239
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thinking that the thoughts... that the speech stands for the thoughts is the same thing as basically saying that the thoughts kind of become speech.

463
00:27:10,379 --> 00:27:14,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, you aren't seeing the... these two separate domains.

464
00:27:14,682 --> 00:27:14,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

465
00:27:15,242 --> 00:27:17,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can ask, well, why is the speech converging point?

466
00:27:17,564 --> 00:27:18,425
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just how things are.

467
00:27:18,745 --> 00:27:19,986
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how the aggregates are ordered.

468
00:27:20,226 --> 00:27:20,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

469
00:27:20,806 --> 00:27:22,107
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not up to you to decide.

470
00:27:22,147 --> 00:27:23,028
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact is, it is.

471
00:27:24,669 --> 00:27:30,314
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as you're confounding it, means there is ignorance in regard to that entire basis.

472
00:27:30,414 --> 00:27:32,876
[SPEAKER_01]: And whenever there is ignorance, there is the automatic appropriation.

473
00:27:34,657 --> 00:27:41,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having sort of distanced yourself from

474
00:27:42,223 --> 00:27:46,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense, desire, sensuality, unwholesome restraints.

475
00:27:47,305 --> 00:27:53,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-distraction, no careless projects and little whatever, however meaningful it might present itself, no little activities.

476
00:27:56,509 --> 00:27:58,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all that is rooted in distraction or sensuality.

477
00:27:58,990 --> 00:27:59,891
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be.

478
00:28:00,291 --> 00:28:02,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you're completely free from those things, in which case, you wouldn't be doing it.

479
00:28:03,793 --> 00:28:07,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So, having practiced that, you also need to practice physical solitude.

480
00:28:08,134 --> 00:28:11,696
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean you, you like, you never see people, you never talk.

481
00:28:11,992 --> 00:28:13,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you talk only with the purpose.

482
00:28:13,833 --> 00:28:14,914
[SPEAKER_01]: The actual purpose.

483
00:28:15,994 --> 00:28:17,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Not pressure or need.

484
00:28:17,996 --> 00:28:18,996
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the purpose.

485
00:28:19,837 --> 00:28:25,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cultivate that, like, as the Buddha said, the monks train themselves in little speech.

486
00:28:26,141 --> 00:28:26,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Speaking little.

487
00:28:27,061 --> 00:28:28,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Put limits on their speech.

488
00:28:28,342 --> 00:28:28,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

489
00:28:28,582 --> 00:28:33,005
[SPEAKER_00]: It's only when necessary.

490
00:28:33,245 --> 00:28:37,948
[SPEAKER_01]: All that is to support your mindfulness of the factual recognition

491
00:28:39,282 --> 00:28:43,985
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a form of thought, but it's a factually correct thought, that you are not speaking.

492
00:28:44,725 --> 00:28:52,989
[SPEAKER_01]: See, my mind can be shouting mad, but I can simultaneously, through that, peripherally discern that my mouth is not uttering any sounds.

493
00:28:53,769 --> 00:28:57,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there is no wrongly assumed connection between my thought...

494
00:28:58,071 --> 00:29:07,756
[SPEAKER_01]: The moment I forget that my mouth is not uttering the sounds, I can very well, basically, immediately jump back.

495
00:29:08,263 --> 00:29:09,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Into the entire... Form a link.

496
00:29:10,164 --> 00:29:10,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

497
00:29:10,784 --> 00:29:11,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

498
00:29:11,664 --> 00:29:13,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Not being aware of it, it's automatic link.

499
00:29:13,845 --> 00:29:14,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

500
00:29:14,205 --> 00:29:16,526
[SPEAKER_01]: It's automatic sort of mess, so to speak.

501
00:29:16,546 --> 00:29:16,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

502
00:29:16,826 --> 00:29:17,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Clutter.

503
00:29:17,366 --> 00:29:17,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

504
00:29:19,046 --> 00:29:19,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

505
00:29:19,367 --> 00:29:26,629
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you dwell long enough, that's why you should cultivate jhānas, don't be negligent, make effort, discern and so on, like in so many suttas.

506
00:29:27,489 --> 00:29:30,290
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you will become so familiar with the...

507
00:29:31,790 --> 00:29:36,492
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess you can call it relationship between the presence of a mouth able to utter sounds

508
00:29:37,059 --> 00:29:48,343
[SPEAKER_01]: In the presence of verbal thinking, the two separate domains, but you will also then fully understand that doesn't matter how careless you are, verbal thoughts can never enter the extent of speech.

509
00:29:49,223 --> 00:29:52,045
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's always thinking and pondering, one breaks into speech.

510
00:29:52,125 --> 00:29:55,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Like literally two simultaneous things, no other form of connection.

511
00:29:57,567 --> 00:30:00,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Any form of connection means form of misconceiving.

512
00:30:04,245 --> 00:30:10,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the not speaking can... it's like... can be the... the background.

513
00:30:11,406 --> 00:30:15,907
[SPEAKER_01]: It is your... the container of your thinking and pondering, the extent of your thinking and pondering.

514
00:30:16,247 --> 00:30:21,988
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as you remain mindful of... again, and the reason why you can remain mindful is because you're not doing it.

515
00:30:22,028 --> 00:30:24,689
[SPEAKER_01]: You are not, I'm mindful of my mouth, I'm mindful of my mouth.

516
00:30:24,809 --> 00:30:24,969
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

517
00:30:25,489 --> 00:30:25,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at all.

518
00:30:26,529 --> 00:30:28,870
[SPEAKER_01]: You're simply mindful of the thought that the mouth is not speaking.

519
00:30:28,930 --> 00:30:30,530
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a negative form of doing.

520
00:30:30,610 --> 00:30:30,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

521
00:30:31,815 --> 00:30:36,359
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, if somebody listens to this, they might think, like, oh, mindful of the sensations in your mouth.

522
00:30:36,539 --> 00:30:36,679
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

523
00:30:37,099 --> 00:30:38,120
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just to know you're not speaking.

524
00:30:38,761 --> 00:30:39,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

525
00:30:39,541 --> 00:30:44,245
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be mindful of all the sensations and still cause the confusion between your thoughts and the domain of speech.

526
00:30:44,265 --> 00:30:44,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

527
00:30:45,006 --> 00:30:46,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not about what you're looking at.

528
00:30:47,248 --> 00:30:51,692
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about the basis of you not making any sounds while there is verbal thinking going on.

529
00:30:53,153 --> 00:30:58,878
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, if... if you've got, like, a clear background, it's this... the fact that I'm not speaking,

530
00:31:00,307 --> 00:31:01,708
[SPEAKER_00]: To whatever it is I'm thinking about.

531
00:31:02,769 --> 00:31:04,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that to me...

532
00:31:04,110 --> 00:31:11,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you lapse within that whatever you're thinking about, you can't lapse for as long as you don't start making sound with your mouth.

533
00:31:11,495 --> 00:31:11,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

534
00:31:11,855 --> 00:31:13,497
[SPEAKER_01]: If your mindfulness has been properly anchored.

535
00:31:13,537 --> 00:31:15,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with, like, mindfulness of the body posture.

536
00:31:16,199 --> 00:31:20,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how much you forget, when you recollect it, you recollect the right order.

537
00:31:20,542 --> 00:31:21,462
[SPEAKER_01]: It was always, yeah, exactly.

538
00:31:21,502 --> 00:31:23,984
[SPEAKER_01]: The posture hasn't changed, the mouth hasn't spoken.

539
00:31:24,044 --> 00:31:24,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

540
00:31:25,465 --> 00:31:27,427
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mouth starts speaking, stop speaking.

541
00:31:28,292 --> 00:31:30,277
[SPEAKER_00]: And you again, re-establish the anchor.

542
00:31:30,397 --> 00:31:34,266
[SPEAKER_00]: But what I was going to say was then, then it's like that background...

543
00:31:35,710 --> 00:31:44,277
[SPEAKER_00]: ...of the fact that I'm not speaking, that provides a background for the domain of thinking and pondering.

544
00:31:44,337 --> 00:31:47,199
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, it basically allows for the unification of mind.

545
00:31:47,219 --> 00:31:47,940
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes clear.

546
00:31:48,020 --> 00:31:49,301
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a form of unification of mind.

547
00:31:49,381 --> 00:31:49,722
[SPEAKER_00]: It's clear.

548
00:31:49,742 --> 00:31:55,967
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the extent of mind, because it's seen against this background, within the fact that I'm not speaking.

549
00:31:56,047 --> 00:32:03,573
[SPEAKER_01]: And thinking and pondering is basically, literally, the entire extent, from anything, from the subtlest thoughts to the most coarse verbal

550
00:32:04,114 --> 00:32:06,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Having a song repeated in your mind that you can't shut down.

551
00:32:06,955 --> 00:32:07,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

552
00:32:07,495 --> 00:32:09,175
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all the domain of thinking and pondering.

553
00:32:10,416 --> 00:32:14,117
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you, based on that, are thinking about your mouth... That's anything that appears.

554
00:32:14,637 --> 00:32:15,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much, yeah.

555
00:32:15,597 --> 00:32:15,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

556
00:32:16,497 --> 00:32:16,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it is.

557
00:32:17,157 --> 00:32:17,358
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

558
00:32:17,598 --> 00:32:20,158
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the extent of the all in that... in that state.

559
00:32:20,278 --> 00:32:26,520
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you fully understand that any dhamma that manifests in the first jhāna is clearly seen

560
00:32:27,313 --> 00:32:31,195
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, all the five aggregates are clearly seen as not mine, not belonging to me.

561
00:32:31,495 --> 00:32:36,098
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why first jhāna is the necessary base for the culmination of wisdom, to be an arahant.

562
00:32:38,799 --> 00:32:43,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, clarity of mindfulness, because it's the clarity of imperturbable order.

563
00:32:44,262 --> 00:32:46,703
[SPEAKER_01]: That this is the container of this.

564
00:32:46,983 --> 00:32:53,147
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's inconceivable, doesn't matter how much I forget, how careless I get, it's inconceivable that this can become that, or vice versa.

565
00:32:54,477 --> 00:32:57,039
[SPEAKER_01]: The domain of bodily remains the container of this.

566
00:33:00,483 --> 00:33:01,403
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just the way it is.

567
00:33:02,324 --> 00:33:03,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

568
00:33:03,746 --> 00:33:17,038
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why, you know, you can... if your mindfulness is developed to the extent necessary on account of, you know, mindfulness of breathing or mindfulness of death, doesn't matter, you can then turn that extent of your mindfulness

569
00:33:17,463 --> 00:33:27,109
[SPEAKER_01]: To the mindfulness of the converging point of natural body... natural engagements between you and the body, so to speak, between the mental and the physical.

570
00:33:27,129 --> 00:33:27,909
[SPEAKER_01]: And establish jhāna.

571
00:33:28,930 --> 00:33:29,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And then take it further.

572
00:33:31,051 --> 00:33:39,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, from the... for the second jhāna, I won't dwell into it too much, because this is more than enough, and people will start thinking about second jhāna without even understanding what the first is.

573
00:33:39,116 --> 00:33:39,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

574
00:33:39,976 --> 00:33:44,759
[SPEAKER_01]: But, from the point of view of the second jhāna, the thinking and pondering is the physical.

575
00:33:46,157 --> 00:33:46,878
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the container.

576
00:33:47,038 --> 00:33:48,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

577
00:33:51,602 --> 00:33:52,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Within which?

578
00:33:55,865 --> 00:34:01,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Unified mind that doesn't... That's why second jhāna is called noble silence, by the way, not the first jhāna.

579
00:34:01,570 --> 00:34:02,591
[SPEAKER_01]: First jhāna, you can't speak.

580
00:34:03,411 --> 00:34:03,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

581
00:34:04,012 --> 00:34:05,253
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not the noble silence.

582
00:34:05,573 --> 00:34:06,334
[SPEAKER_01]: No, noble silence.

583
00:34:06,354 --> 00:34:10,657
[SPEAKER_01]: The silence is when the mind stops speaking as well.

584
00:34:10,858 --> 00:34:14,901
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you want to stop thinking, you've got to stop speaking with your mind, which is the second jhāna.

585
00:34:16,259 --> 00:34:18,020
[SPEAKER_01]: And you won't get there until you get the first one.

586
00:34:19,621 --> 00:34:23,423
[SPEAKER_01]: And you won't get the first one for as long as you think you can get it, as a thing.

587
00:34:26,325 --> 00:34:28,706
[SPEAKER_01]: You can get it by cultivating the basis for it.

588
00:34:29,787 --> 00:34:30,727
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a consequence.

589
00:34:30,787 --> 00:34:32,048
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a consequence, exactly.

590
00:34:32,148 --> 00:34:35,170
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, if it were not consequence, it would be something you're doing.

591
00:34:35,390 --> 00:34:35,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

592
00:34:36,130 --> 00:34:42,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And then we're going to say, like, oh, you can't say in jhāna, I am entering the first jhāna, I'm practicing the first jhāna, I'm obtaining the first jhāna, I'm leaving the first jhāna.

593
00:34:47,725 --> 00:34:49,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean you're not aware of anything.

594
00:34:49,546 --> 00:34:50,767
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the reason why you can't say.

595
00:34:51,388 --> 00:34:53,089
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't say, because it's... you're not doing it.

596
00:34:53,930 --> 00:34:58,093
[SPEAKER_01]: It's result of things you've done, such as surmounting sensuality.

597
00:34:59,473 --> 00:35:05,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Anchoring your peripheral recollectedness in the fact that factually mouth is abstaining, not speaking, not uttering sounds.

598
00:35:06,718 --> 00:35:08,239
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's been doing so for a while.

599
00:35:08,719 --> 00:35:19,487
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, you know, like, having... having been secluded from sensuality, sensual pleasure, a joy from seclusion arises, one abides in jhāna, having acquired it.

600
00:35:19,907 --> 00:35:25,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean, you know, as I said before, like, maybe took you a whole month to surmount the sensual domain.

601
00:35:27,313 --> 00:35:30,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Before then, the result of your abiding in that state becomes the jhāna.

602
00:35:30,756 --> 00:35:31,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Or it can take you half a day.

603
00:35:32,818 --> 00:35:35,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes more, sometimes less, depending how careless you were, if you were careless.

604
00:35:36,582 --> 00:35:39,164
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, once you cultivate it long enough, it's done at will.

605
00:35:40,285 --> 00:35:41,126
[SPEAKER_01]: As the suttas would say.

606
00:35:41,486 --> 00:35:47,672
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, it's not... See, you know, whenever they would say, oh, I don't need all of this, because whoever can obtain jhānas, it will.

607
00:35:48,444 --> 00:35:51,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Like when Mahākassapa said that, for example, I don't need all these luxuries.

608
00:35:52,247 --> 00:35:52,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, yeah.

609
00:35:52,788 --> 00:35:56,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Because as far as we can obtain jhānas at will, he's like the greatest, basically.

610
00:35:56,091 --> 00:35:57,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

611
00:35:58,893 --> 00:36:00,114
[SPEAKER_01]: He's saying that as a matter of fact.

612
00:36:00,175 --> 00:36:05,980
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why there is a praise, oh, you can obtain jhānas at will, is because he's done all the work.

613
00:36:07,561 --> 00:36:11,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you wouldn't... Well, mind you, even a mind would take praise on account of anything.

614
00:36:12,407 --> 00:36:18,273
[SPEAKER_01]: But a rational mind, mind that is authentic, will recognize, well, there is a praise of random things that you have nothing to do with.

615
00:36:18,293 --> 00:36:18,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

616
00:36:18,674 --> 00:36:19,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, look how tall you are.

617
00:36:19,955 --> 00:36:20,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

618
00:36:20,216 --> 00:36:21,237
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no say in that.

619
00:36:21,941 --> 00:36:23,542
[SPEAKER_01]: But look how virtuous you are.

620
00:36:24,142 --> 00:36:31,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Look how well kind of mannered or well-read or intelligent and reasonable you are, because you made the effort.

621
00:36:31,387 --> 00:36:32,748
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is a legitimate praise.

622
00:36:32,768 --> 00:36:37,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you invested... jhānas is the utmost effort you can make, in that sense.

623
00:36:37,311 --> 00:36:38,411
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's the best praise.

624
00:36:38,952 --> 00:36:42,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Whoever can attain it with will, means not he was lucky to get them.

625
00:36:43,054 --> 00:36:51,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Means he done so... such an extent of this anchoring mindfulness proper work, that literally, at will, everything is there.

626
00:36:54,020 --> 00:36:57,722
[SPEAKER_01]: No, because he just, you know, won the jackpot and it just happens to him.

627
00:36:57,863 --> 00:36:59,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he's so lucky.

628
00:36:59,223 --> 00:37:00,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you praise that?

629
00:37:00,584 --> 00:37:02,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you're lucky and nothing to do with you.

630
00:37:08,690 --> 00:37:19,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So anchoring your mindfulness in the recognition of a mouth that is not uttering sounds.

631
00:37:23,869 --> 00:37:24,930
[SPEAKER_01]: That is a converging point.

632
00:37:25,510 --> 00:37:33,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind you, when it comes to speech, writing, typing, all those would be means of physical converging of your expression.

633
00:37:34,358 --> 00:37:36,380
[SPEAKER_01]: But the chief of them is the mind.

634
00:37:40,663 --> 00:37:45,029
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, again, sensuality is inherently inseparable from the notion of your mouth as well.

635
00:37:45,690 --> 00:37:51,178
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the first point of engagement with sensuality when you're a toddler, breastfeeding, eating.

636
00:37:51,498 --> 00:37:54,823
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the first point of satisfaction and it always remains.

637
00:37:55,476 --> 00:38:00,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, as I said, your sensuality can proliferate, your preferences can proliferate, the expression of your real will can proliferate.

638
00:38:01,100 --> 00:38:03,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, it's all still within the mouth.

639
00:38:03,242 --> 00:38:05,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Just its density keeps increasing the more you engage with it.

640
00:38:06,084 --> 00:38:13,129
[SPEAKER_01]: But it cannot become anywhere else, except from the container of the mouth, because that's the first point of engagement with the body.

641
00:38:13,169 --> 00:38:14,990
[SPEAKER_01]: The first point of confusion, so to speak.

642
00:38:15,030 --> 00:38:17,612
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a convergence between mind and body.

643
00:38:18,331 --> 00:38:19,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind is there, body is there.

644
00:38:19,991 --> 00:38:24,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, for you, mouth is kind of... pertains to the mental and pertains to the physical.

645
00:38:24,752 --> 00:38:26,673
[SPEAKER_01]: How is that possible?

646
00:38:27,933 --> 00:38:31,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Not fully clarifying that, that's how you own both.

647
00:38:31,694 --> 00:38:33,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Or that's how you get affected by both.

648
00:38:34,515 --> 00:38:39,356
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, that's why the recognition of that... that is actually impossible.

649
00:38:39,476 --> 00:38:45,237
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable for... doesn't matter how verbal thinking is, it's inconceivable for it to become the sounds from your mouth.

650
00:38:46,323 --> 00:38:49,666
[SPEAKER_01]: The way it happens is just on this level, on these two simultaneous levels.

651
00:38:49,686 --> 00:38:49,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

652
00:38:50,266 --> 00:39:01,675
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you fully understand that it doesn't matter what kind of thoughts you had, how threatening, fearful, you will not be afraid, nor threatened, because you realize, oh, that's where this is manifested, and it's actually inconceivable.

653
00:39:01,895 --> 00:39:02,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

654
00:39:03,536 --> 00:39:05,517
[SPEAKER_00]: And so now, that's going back to, so...

655
00:39:06,158 --> 00:39:07,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with your will, by the way.

656
00:39:07,379 --> 00:39:07,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Restraint.

657
00:39:08,159 --> 00:39:10,601
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not to... we were talking... you talked about bodily restraint.

658
00:39:13,784 --> 00:39:13,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Erm...

659
00:39:17,067 --> 00:39:21,989
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, having thoughts of ill will, say, but just making sure that you don't do anything.

660
00:39:22,009 --> 00:39:27,571
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you're sort of describing the... how to restrain yourself on the verbal level.

661
00:39:27,991 --> 00:39:28,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

662
00:39:28,592 --> 00:39:29,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Which then restrains your mind.

663
00:39:31,053 --> 00:39:41,157
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason your mind was not restrained is because it was under the assumption that it can pertain to that which concerns me, which is the external world of the senses.

664
00:39:41,805 --> 00:39:45,369
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize the mind can never step into it, nor can it become the mind either way.

665
00:39:45,389 --> 00:39:50,835
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just physical, mental, bodily, and on the level of the mind.

666
00:39:51,896 --> 00:39:57,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure, fundamentally, it's all mental, in terms of it's all dhamma manifesting itself, but that's a different approach.

667
00:39:58,824 --> 00:39:59,945
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the approach of jhāna.

668
00:40:04,522 --> 00:40:09,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is about understanding this converging point between...

669
00:40:09,863 --> 00:40:15,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Points of engagement with the body, which is a necessary basis for any sensuality to affect you, to pressure you, to pertain to you.

670
00:40:19,445 --> 00:40:31,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when that order becomes uncorruptible, by your ignoring, by your carelessness, you're abiding in that which you've acquired, which is the right order, which is where jhāna is.

671
00:40:32,413 --> 00:40:33,673
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they're always wholesome.

672
00:40:33,773 --> 00:40:35,074
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have unwholesome jhāna.

673
00:40:35,434 --> 00:40:35,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

674
00:40:36,234 --> 00:40:40,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Because unwholesomeness is defined, as we said before in other talks, by the perversion of the order.

675
00:40:41,015 --> 00:40:43,216
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a definite... That's why unwholesome things are unwholesome.

676
00:40:43,636 --> 00:40:45,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they contain a contradiction.

677
00:40:47,237 --> 00:40:48,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Repulsive as attractive.

678
00:40:52,838 --> 00:40:53,838
[SPEAKER_01]: In jhāna, that's impossible.

679
00:40:54,218 --> 00:40:55,999
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's impossible for them to be unwholesome.

680
00:40:56,519 --> 00:40:58,319
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, again, in first jhāna...

681
00:41:00,220 --> 00:41:01,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we said at the beginning that

682
00:41:03,178 --> 00:41:09,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Overcoming sensuality involves putting the body first, recognizing, not putting it first, but seeing it, seeing that it is first.

683
00:41:09,240 --> 00:41:10,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing that it is first, discerning it.

684
00:41:11,121 --> 00:41:14,142
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a necessary... and not like first initially and then change it.

685
00:41:14,222 --> 00:41:15,163
[SPEAKER_01]: It remains first.

686
00:41:15,583 --> 00:41:15,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

687
00:41:15,823 --> 00:41:19,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter the extent of sensual pressure, engagement, it remains first.

688
00:41:19,604 --> 00:41:20,265
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't not be.

689
00:41:20,425 --> 00:41:24,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that, you discern even a more specific manifestation of that order of the body.

690
00:41:24,766 --> 00:41:26,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is again, body first, which is the...

691
00:41:26,187 --> 00:41:26,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mouth.

692
00:41:26,807 --> 00:41:27,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

693
00:41:27,667 --> 00:41:28,208
[SPEAKER_01]: And your thoughts.

694
00:41:28,428 --> 00:41:28,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

695
00:41:29,626 --> 00:41:32,287
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's still that... Because that's exactly what sanctuary is.

696
00:41:32,367 --> 00:41:33,027
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the container.

697
00:41:33,047 --> 00:41:35,068
[SPEAKER_01]: The body and the thoughts.

698
00:41:35,528 --> 00:41:35,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

699
00:41:36,428 --> 00:41:38,649
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to everything that the body can go for.

700
00:41:43,090 --> 00:41:46,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Rather than the usual kind of, I use my body or I...

701
00:41:46,931 --> 00:41:47,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

702
00:41:55,274 --> 00:41:59,075
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way that that mindfulness of presently enduring

703
00:42:00,460 --> 00:42:01,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-speaking mouth.

704
00:42:03,243 --> 00:42:07,928
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way that can become intelligible is on the basis of thorough sense restraint.

705
00:42:09,490 --> 00:42:09,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

706
00:42:10,712 --> 00:42:14,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Celibacy, withdrawal from sensuality, ill-will.

707
00:42:15,977 --> 00:42:18,120
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you get to understand all the other hindrances as well.

708
00:42:19,341 --> 00:42:20,463
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, that doesn't matter how much

709
00:42:21,382 --> 00:42:31,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Sleepiness, restlessness might be like... how heavy it might be, you won't be bearing that weight for as long as you don't pervert the aura of what comes first, which is the body.

710
00:42:32,230 --> 00:42:37,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So this is... whether you want it or not, it cannot be anywhere else except on the body, the domain.

